pnptask simulation config
=========================

A runnable pick-and-place machine for the pnptask module: a 600 x 500 mm XYZ
gantry with two pickers, two tray stations, two process stations and two
dead-zone drawings. Everything is simulated — position feedback is looped back
from the command and the home switches are always made — so it runs anywhere,
with no hardware and no realtime privileges.

    cd tests/pnptask
    stmakd pnptask.ini

    halcmd show pin pnp.task        # the whole HAL interface
    halcmd show param pnp.task      # the tuning knobs (settle times, offsets)

The machine comes up enabled (machine-on is held high in the HAL file) and runs
jobs by hand. Tell it what the trays hold, then command one:

    halcmd setp pnp.task.process-step 0
    halcmd setp pnp.task.tray.10.set-full 1   # tray 10 is full of step-0 parts

    halcmd setp pnp.task.origin-id 10         # component tray
    halcmd setp pnp.task.dest-id   20         # press
    halcmd setp pnp.task.start-job 1          # the module clears it when done

    halcmd show pin pnp.task.error-id         # 0 = the job worked
    halcmd show pin pnp.task.proc.20.has-material

    halcmd setp pnp.task.start-job 0          # then 20 -> 11 empties the press
    halcmd setp pnp.task.origin-id 20
    halcmd setp pnp.task.dest-id   11
    halcmd setp pnp.task.start-job 1

The config loads with pickers=2, so the alternating picker of §8 is active.
Commanding 10 -> 20 a second time, with the press still holding the first part,
runs a swap: the free picker takes the finished part out, the other one puts the
new one in, and the press never stands empty. The removed part is then in a
picker, and the next job has to be the one that carries it away — any other
origin is refused with error-id 19 (ALT_PICKER_SEQUENCE):

    halcmd setp pnp.task.origin-id 10 ; halcmd setp pnp.task.dest-id 20
    halcmd setp pnp.task.start-job 0 ; halcmd setp pnp.task.start-job 1

    halcmd show pin pnp.task.picker.1.close   # the swapped-out part is held here

    halcmd setp pnp.task.origin-id 20 ; halcmd setp pnp.task.dest-id 21
    halcmd setp pnp.task.start-job 0 ; halcmd setp pnp.task.start-job 1
                                              # no pick: the part is already held

The first job homes the machine itself (AUTOHOME = 1). To home it and jog it
around by hand instead:

    halcmd setp pnp.task.home 1     # rising edge: home all joints
    halcmd setp pnp.task.home 0
    halcmd show pin pnp.task.homed

    halcmd setp pnp.task.auto-enable 0   # manual mode
    halcmd setp pnp.task.jog-speed 100
    halcmd setp pnp.task.jog-x-pos 1
    halcmd setp pnp.task.jog-x-pos 0

    halcmd show pin pnp.task.picker      # picker.N.pos-x/pos-y follow the jog

Files
-----

    pnptask.ini      machine + [PNPTASK*] configuration
    pnptask.hal      motion stack, the task, and the simulated machine
    zones.dxf        travel envelope and permanent obstacles (deadzone-select 0)
    zones_alt.dxf    the same envelope plus a fixture plate (deadzone-select 1)
    pnpdrv.sh        server lifecycle for the scenario tests
    pnpdrv.py        pin access and job helpers for the scenario drivers
    db/              persisted state (scratch — delete it for a fresh machine)

The drawings are read in machine units, the same coordinates the INI stations
are taught in. Layer "Outer Limits" carries the envelope, layer "deadzones" the
obstacles; both must be closed and convex.

The envelope is drawn wider than the axis soft limits (-20..620 x -20..520
against 0..600 x 0..500) on purpose. CLEARANCE erodes it by 12 mm, and a route's
start point has to lie inside the eroded boundary — including wherever the
machine happens to be parked when a job starts, which after homing is the corner
of the axis range.

The layout
----------

    tray station 10   component tray   TRAYDEF 1: 10 x 4 grid, (100,380)..(280,440)
    tray station 11   output tray      TRAYDEF 2: single position at (60,60)
    proc station 20   press            (300,200), waits out "busy" at (250,120)
    proc station 21   test             (450,250)
    dead zone         frame post       (340,300)..(420,370)
    dead zone         cable anchor     (500,60)..(560,140)
    dead zone         fixture plate    (150,150)..(220,260)   [zones_alt.dxf only]

Which tray geometry a tray station uses is a runtime choice: set its tray-id pin
to a TRAYDEF ID. Both drawings are validated against every taught position when
the module loads, so a station moved into an obstacle fails the load rather than
a job.

What the simulated machine does
-------------------------------

The field devices are two `pnpsim` instances (pnpsim(9)), one per
picker/process-station pair: instance 0 carries picker 0's gripper and station
20's fixture, instance 1 carries picker 1's and station 21's. Out of the box
every close finds a part, every fixture answers its release request after a few
milliseconds, and neither station is ever busy — a machine that runs jobs.

The knobs that make it misbehave on purpose are pins, so a test (or you, with
halcmd) can flip them between jobs:

    halcmd setp pnp.sim.0.gripper.miss-count 2   # the next 2 closes grip nothing
    halcmd setp pnp.sim.0.gripper.miss-count -1  # ... and -1 means every close
    halcmd setp pnp.sim.0.gripper.stuck 1        # a gripper that never actuates
    halcmd setp pnp.sim.0.fixture.stuck 1        # a clamp that never answers
    halcmd setp pnp.task.proc.20.busy 1          # the station is working

A miss run of -1 walks the tray until MAX_UNPOPULATED successive picks have
found nothing, which is how the tray declares itself empty (error-id 11).

Each tray station's tray-id is set in the HAL file, which a PLC would do when a
tray is loaded. The job handshake itself (process-step, origin-id, dest-id,
start-job) is left unconnected, because that is what a PLC drives and what the
commands above poke by hand.

Tray contents, station occupancy and held material are persisted (into db/ next
to this file, by persist_sqlite) and restored at the next start. Delete db/ for
a machine that comes up with empty trays and empty stations.

The scenario directories
------------------------

    cycle/      a whole pick -> place cycle, and D13's plan-time budget
    probing/    empty slots corrected by the gripper, up to TRAY_EMPTY (D9)
    homing/     the home request and manual jog (D18, D25)
    busy/       busy gating, the wait position, the auto-enable abort (D15)
    errors/     the error ids of §7.5 and the error-reset that clears them
    estop/      an estop mid-job and the way back to a running machine (D14)
    persist/    the tracked world surviving a restart (D6)
    altpicker/  the alternating-picker reference flow of §8
    manual/     the manual-handling retention round trip of §8.1

Each is a `runtests` test: a test.sh that starts stmakd on this config and a
Python driver that commands jobs over the handshake pins and asserts what came
back. They share pnpdrv.sh (server lifecycle) and pnpdrv.py (pin access over
the halcmd REST API) from this directory. Run them all with

    scripts/runtests tests/pnptask

This directory itself holds no test.hal/test.sh, so `runtests` ignores it: it is
the config the scenarios drive, and a machine to run by hand.
